    steps: [
      { text: "The whole machine: a model that only emits text, tools that act, and a harness running the loop. Fleets fork or wrap a standard one: goose at Stripe, OpenCode at Ramp, the Claude Agent SDK at Spotify." },
      { text: "The harness builds context: system prompt, tool definitions, rules files such as `AGENTS.md`, then your task. Every tool definition costs context, so fleets give each run a curated subset.", on: ["user", "rules", "ctx"], flow: ["e-task", "e-rules"] },
      { text: "Each turn resends the whole context, the agent's only working memory: a median Shopify River session is 19 minutes and 50 tool calls, each result growing the window.", on: ["ctx", "model"], flow: ["e-read"] },
      { text: "The model replies with tool calls or a final answer. That choice is the judgement, and it is nondeterministic: rerun the task and the path and diff can change.", on: ["model", "toolq"], flow: ["e-reply"] },
      { text: "The runner checks policy (prompt, allowlist, classifier, sandbox), then edits files, runs commands or calls MCP. Stripe skips prompts: minions get full permissions because a mistake is \"confined to the limited blast radius of one devbox\".", on: ["toolq", "runner", "files", "shell", "mcp"], flow: ["e-yes", "e-x1", "e-x2", "e-x3"] },
      { text: "Determinism lives here: tests, builds and linters give the same verdict for the same code, what Anthropic calls ground truth from the environment at every step.", on: ["shell", "runner"], flow: ["e-x2"] },
      { text: "Results are appended and the loop reruns; near the limit old tool output is cleared, then summarized. The transcript is the state: Shopify's append-only Postgres log is \"the canonical truth\", and the harness \"cheap to recreate, disposable\".", on: ["runner", "ctx"], flow: ["e-obs"] },
      { text: "No tool call means the model thinks it is done, and without an outside check that is the only signal. Limits apply (turns, time, tokens, Esc); Stripe allows two CI rounds, then a human gets the branch: \"CI runs cost tokens, compute, and time\".", on: ["toolq", "gate"], flow: ["e-no"] },
      { text: "A stop check can refuse the stop: a Claude Code hook exiting with code 2 blocks it and returns the error to context. Spotify's stop hook runs verifiers opaque to the agent: \"If one of the verifiers fails, the PR isn't opened\".", on: ["gate", "ctx"], flow: ["e-retry"] },
      { text: "When checks pass, the run ends with evidence and a branch, not a merge: GitHub Copilot cloud agent pushes to one branch, can't approve or merge its own PR, and signs commits that link to its session log.", on: ["gate", "result"], flow: ["e-done"] },
      { text: "In Anthropic's terms: in a workflow, code fixes the path; in an agent, the model picks each step. Stripe's blueprints mix both: code nodes (\"Run configured linters\", \"Push changes\") between agent nodes (\"Implement task\", \"Fix CI failures\").", on: ["model", "toolq", "runner"] },
    ],
    say30: "An agent is a model, tools and a loop, run by a harness. The harness puts instructions, rules and the task in context; the model calls a tool or answers; tool calls pass a policy check and their results go back into context. Judgement is in the model and nondeterministic; determinism is in tests, builds and linters. So I wrap the loop in code it can't skip, like Stripe's blueprints: lint and push as plain code, two CI rounds then a human. The boundary is the environment, not a prompt, and done is a check, not the model's word.",
    gotchas: [
      "Unattended judgement still misfires: Steve Yegge runs agents under 100+ \"fences\", yet every morning after a night left alone, Claude Fable \"has made at least one terrible decision\".",
      "A prompt is not a boundary: Anthropic's 2026 cyber-eval prompts told models they had no internet access, but a vendor environment had it, and Claude Opus 4.7 got into a real company's database with \"several hundred rows of production data\".",
      "Merging is a harness choice. Most fleets forbid it, but OpenAI's harness team (3 engineers, later 7; ~1,500 merged PRs in five months) lets agents \"often squash and merge their own pull requests\" on an internal beta.",
      "Same prompt, different diff: judge yield, not demos. Shopify's River logged 59,918 sessions and coauthored 3,536 merged PRs in 30 days (about 6%; sessions include non-PR work).",
    ],
    atScale: [
      "Cost is per run: every turn resends the context. Bun's 11-day Zig-to-Rust port, about 64 Claudes at peak, cost around $165K at API prices; one Lovable engineer spent about $85K over January to June 2026, $25K in May.",
      "Tool calls are the load: Cursor's research harness peaked at about 1,000 commits an hour, across 10M tool calls in one week.",
      "Runaway loops: cap turns (`max_turns` raises `MaxTurnsExceeded` in the OpenAI Agents SDK; Claude Code subagents take `maxTurns`) and CI rounds. Alert on cap hits.",
      "Prompts don't survive unattended runs, so the boundary moves to the environment: Monzo deploys each job into a sandbox \"well away from all the services we use to run the bank\".",
      "Laptops cap the loop: Ramp's handled 1 to 2 sessions each. Fleets go remote: DoorDash gives each run a Firecracker microVM with repos, tools and scoped secrets, targeting p95 setup under 5 seconds.",
    ],
    quiz: [
      { q: "What are the parts of an agent, and which does the harness own?", a: "A model, tools and a loop. The harness owns the loop: context, model calls, policy, tool runs, appended results, compaction, stop conditions. Fleets reuse one: Stripe forked goose; Spotify wraps the Claude Agent SDK." },
      { q: "Why can Stripe's minions run with full permissions and no prompts?", a: "The boundary is the environment: each run gets its own devbox in a QA environment with no real user data, production services or arbitrary egress. The minion pushes a branch; engineers review before merge." },
      { q: "An unattended run never ends. What should have stopped it?", a: "Harness limits (`max_turns`, a subagent `maxTurns`, a timeout, a token budget), a cap on CI rounds, and a deterministic stop check that defines done." },
    ],
    code: [
      { lang: "python", label: "the loop inside a blueprint", src: "ctx = [system, rules, task]\nfor ci_round in range(2):            # then a human\n    for turn in range(MAX_TURNS):\n        reply = model(ctx, tools=TOOLS)\n        if not reply.tool_calls: break\n        for c in reply.tool_calls:\n            ctx.append(execute(c) if permitted(c)\n                       else denied(c))\n    run_linters(); push()             # code, no LLM\n    ok, log = run_ci_and_verifiers()\n    if ok: return open_pr(evidence=log)\n    ctx.append(log)                   # fix and retry\nreturn hand_to_human(branch)" },
    ],
