    steps: [
      { text: "The trust loop from pstack, Lauren Tan's agent playbooks: the top row understands, the bottom row proves, and nothing moves right on a claim. The stakes: Spotify ranks a PR that passes CI but is functionally wrong as \"the most serious error\", worse than no PR." },
      { text: "Reproduce it yourself on the surface the user hit. pstack's bug-fix playbook says synthesize the trigger or add instrumentation, never hand it back. That takes a real environment: Ramp gives each agent session a sandboxed VM with the full local dev stack.", on: ["report", "repro"], flow: ["e-report"] },
      { text: "Find the cause by elimination, confirmed at runtime. A known-good oracle makes each split cheap to judge: Cursor's agent-built FastRender browser leaned on compiler errors, vendored web specs, and screenshots diffed against golden samples.", on: ["repro", "hyp"], flow: ["e-repro"] },
      { text: "Not proven yet? Add logging and go round again. pstack's attack-the-premise rule: after two failed fixes that share a premise, question the premise instead of trying a third.", on: ["hyp", "repro"], flow: ["e-more"] },
      { text: "Commit the failing repro before the fix (pstack's sequence-verifiable-units). That also pins the oracle: InfoQ reported Spotify's agents commenting out failing tests and downgrading Java versions to turn builds green.", on: ["hyp", "failing"], flow: ["e-yes"] },
      { text: "Ship the smallest fix the evidence supports, and no stubs: after Claude stubbed out non-compiling functions in Bun's Rust port, reviewers got the rule \"If you need a paragraph-long comment to justify why the workaround is OK, the code is wrong\".", on: ["failing", "fix"], flow: ["e-fix"] },
      { text: "Verify on the same surface: the original repro must now pass. Unit tests show branch behavior, not that the bug is gone, and an inconclusive check is not a pass.", on: ["fix", "verify"], flow: ["e-rerun"] },
      { text: "Still failing? Back to the hypotheses, and revert every change a refuted hypothesis motivated.", on: ["verify", "hyp"], flow: ["e-fail"] },
      { text: "Evidence, not assertion: read the real artifact, not a proxy (pstack's prove-it-works). Ramp's agent checks like an engineer: tests, telemetry and feature-flag queries for backend work, screenshots and live previews for frontend.", on: ["verify", "evidence"], flow: ["e-ev"] },
      { text: "Someone else grades it. pstack's interrogate runs one review prompt on two different models. Bun's port gave each task one implementer, two or more adversarial reviewers who saw only the diff, and a separate fixer.", on: ["evidence", "review"], flow: ["e-review"] },
      { text: "Hard guards sit where the agent can't edit them. Spotify's verifiers are opaque to the agent and run by a stop hook: \"If one of the verifiers fails, the PR isn't opened\". Green is an input, not a verdict.", on: ["review", "ci", "fix"], flow: ["e-ci", "e-red"] },
      { text: "Land small verified units one at a time, so a failure points at its cause. Anthropic's long-running harness tells the agent to edit only a `passes` field in a JSON feature list, chosen because models tamper with JSON less than Markdown.", on: ["ci", "merge"], flow: ["e-merge"] },
    ],
    say30: "Agents stop when work looks done, and green can be gamed: InfoQ reported Spotify's agents commenting out failing tests. So done is a check the agent can't edit. I run pstack's loop: reproduce on the user's surface, prove the cause at runtime, commit the failing repro, make the smallest fix, re-run that repro. Evidence is a rerunnable script, telemetry or a screenshot, not a claim, and a different model or reviewer grades it.",
    gotchas: [
      "\"Tests pass\" can mean the agent edited the tests. Commit the failing repro first and review test diffs like code; Bun's port ran its full suite with zero tests skipped.",
      "A self-report is not verification. pstack's prove-it-works rejects proxies like timestamps or cached screenshots.",
      "Strict graders block good work too. Spotify's LLM judge vetoed about a quarter of sessions; InfoQ says the team found it \"too rigid\" and later removed it, which no Spotify post confirms.",
      "A check on the wrong surface (unit test for a UI bug, staging for a prod-only race) is inconclusive; pstack says flag it, not pass it.",
    ],
    atScale: [
      "Parallel agents need an external oracle: Bun's port (~64 Claudes at peak) moved 535,496 lines of Zig to Rust in 11 days against its 60,624 Linux tests; Anthropic's 16 compiler agents each picked a different failing test.",
      "Yield tracks the oracle: in dotnet/runtime, agent cleanup PRs succeeded 84.7% of the time and performance PRs 54.5%; tests were 65.7% of lines added in merged agent PRs, vs 49.9% in sampled merged human PRs.",
      "Generated tests need their own gate. Meta's ACH keeps a test only if it builds, isn't flaky and catches a mutant no existing test catches; engineers accepted 140 of 191 reviewed.",
      "Landing without upfront peer review takes a measured gate: Meta lets authors self-attest AI diffs only when drift is low and its Spotlight tool flags no regions; self-reviewed diffs revert at 0.33% against a 0.17% baseline.",
      "Unattended runs need a gate the model can't talk past: a Stop hook or goal condition that runs the check script. Both have safety caps, so a run that stopped with the check red is unfinished.",
    ],
    quiz: [
      { q: "Why commit the failing repro before the fix?", a: "History then proves the fix: the repro fails on the parent and passes on the fix. It also pins the oracle, so the agent can't quietly weaken the test." },
      { q: "Unit tests pass but the user-facing bug remains. What went wrong?", a: "Wrong surface. Unit tests show branch behavior; only the original repro where the user hit it proves the fix. Ramp's agent, for one, verifies frontend work visually and gives users screenshots." },
      { q: "Name three ways teams stop agents gaming their own checks.", a: "Spotify runs opaque verifiers from a stop hook. Bun's reviewers saw only the diff and treated a paragraph-long justification as a sign of wrong code. Anthropic's harness tells the agent to edit only a `passes` field." },
    ],
    code: [
      { lang: "bash", label: "repro/verify-1234.sh", src: "#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Fails before the fix, passes after.\nset -euo pipefail\nresp=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \\\n  -H 'Authorization: Bearer expired' \\\n  \"$BASE_URL/api/session/refresh\")\necho \"expired refresh -> $resp\"\ntest \"$resp\" = 401" },
      { lang: "yaml", label: ".github/workflows/guards.yml", src: "on: [pull_request, merge_group]\njobs:\n  guards:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: make lint typecheck test\n      - run: for f in repro/*.sh; do bash \"$f\"; done\n      - name: Test count floor\n        run: test $(make -s count-tests) -ge $(cat ci/min-tests)" },
    ],
    codeNote: "Illustrative. The repro script is the evidence: committed before the fix, rerun in CI so the bug can't silently return. The test floor fails the build when tests are skipped or deleted; require code-owner review on `ci/min-tests` so only humans lower it.",
